===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{C|en|Bodily functions}} {{lb|en|intransitive}} To draw [[air]] into ([[inhale]]), and expel air from ([[exhale]]), the [[lung]]s in order to extract [[oxygen]] and [[excrete]] waste gases.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To take in needed gases and expel waste gases in a similar way.
#: {{ux|en|Fish have gills so they can '''breathe''' underwater.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To inhale (a gas) to sustain life.
#: {{ux|en|While life as we know it depends on oxygen, scientists have speculated that alien life forms might '''breathe''' chlorine or methane.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}} To [[live]].
#: {{ux|en|I will not allow it, as long as I still '''breathe'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|II|i|passage=I am in health, I '''breathe'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Lay of the Last Minstrel
|passage='''Breathes''' there a man with soul so dead?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To draw something into the lungs.
#: {{ux|en|Try not to '''breathe''' too much smoke.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To expel air from the lungs, [[exhale]].
#: {{ux|en|If you '''breathe''' on a mirror, it will fog up.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[exhale]] or [[expel]] (something) in the manner of [[breath]].
#: {{ux|en|The flowers '''breathed''' a heady perfume.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Timothy Groves|title=The Book Of Creatures|isbn=1105473058|page=85
|passage=Mountain Drakes '''breathe''' fire, Ice Drakes '''breathe''' ice, Swamp Drakes '''breathe''' acid, and Forest Drakes '''breathe''' lightning.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To give an [[impression]] of, to [[exude]].
#: {{ux|en|The decor positively '''breathes''' classical elegance.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[whisper]] quietly.
#: {{ux|en|He '''breathed''' the words into her ear, but she understood them all.}}
# To pass like breath; noiselessly or gently; to emanate; to blow gently.
#: {{ux|en|The wind '''breathes''' through the trees.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|2|1|passage=The air '''breathes''' upon us here most sweetly.}}
#* {{RQ:Byron Childe Harold|passage=There '''breathes''' a living fragrance from the shore.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|Evangelical and Charismatic|_|Christianity|with God as agent}} To [[inspire]] ([[scripture]]).
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1850|author=John Howard Hinton|title=On the Divine Inspiration of the Scriptures. A lecture, etc|page=16
|passage=The affirmation before us, then, will be, "All scripture is divinely '''breathed'''."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1917|author=J. C. Ferdinand Pittman|title=Bible Truths Illustrated: For the Use of Preachers, Teachers, Bible-school, Christian Endeavor, Temperance and Other Christian Workers|page=168
|passage={{...}} that God, who '''breathed''' the Scriptures, &quot;cannot lie,&quot; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Jay E. Adams|title=The Christian Counselor's Manual: The Practice of Nouthetic Counseling|publisher=Zondervan|isbn=9780310871736
|passage=Paul says that since God '''breathed''' the Scriptures, they are therefore useful; he did not put it the other way around (i.e., that they are useful, therefore inspired).}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To exchange gases with the environment.
#: {{ux|en|Uncork the wine and leave it on the table for a few minutes to allow it to '''breathe'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of a material etc., to allow gases to pass through.
#: {{ux|en|Garments made of certain new materials '''breathe''' well and keep the skin relatively dry during exercise.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|now|rare}} To [[rest]]; to stop and catch one's breath.
#* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|12.64|2|X|lxiiij|passage=Thenne they lasshed to gyder many sad strokes / & tracyd and trauercyd now bakward / now sydelyng hurtlyng to gyders lyke two bores / & that same tyme they felle both grouelyng to the erthe / Thus they fought styll withoute ony reposynge two houres and neuer '''brethed'''}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1|2|4
|passage=Well! '''breathe''' awhile, and then to it again!}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To stop, to give {{qualifier|a horse}} an opportunity to catch its breath.
#: {{ux|en|At higher altitudes you need to '''breathe''' your horse more often.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[exercise]]; to [[tire]] by [[brisk]] exercise.
# {{lb|en|transitive|figurative}} To [[passionate]]ly [[devote]] much of one's life to (an activity, etc.).
#: {{ux|en|―Do you like hiking? ―Are you kidding? I '''breathe''' hiking.}}

