{{en-verb|~s|~ing|built,~ed<l:archaic,or,poetic>}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[form]] (something) by combining [[material]]s or [[part]]s.
#: {{ux|en|I '''built''' a shed in my backyard.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1596.2|book=IV|canto=X|stanza=6|page=140|passage=It was a bridge '''ybuilt''' in goodly wize, / With curious Corbes and pendants grauen faire, [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|chapter=Prologue|page=16|passage=Athelstan Arundel walked home all the way, foaming and raging. No omnibus, cab, or conveyance ever '''built''' could contain a young man in such a rage. His mother lived at Pembridge Square, which is four good measured miles from Lincoln's Inn.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=A chap named Eleazir Kendrick and I had chummed in together the summer afore and '''built''' a fish-weir and shanty at Setuckit Point, down Orham way. For a spell we done pretty well.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[develop]] or give form to (something) according to a [[plan]] or [[process]].
#: {{ux|en|He will '''build''' an organization equipped for success.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-21|author=w:Oliver Burkeman|volume=189|issue=2|page=27|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/technology/2013/may/10/conscious-computing-twitter-facebook-google|title=The tao of tech|passage=The dirty secret of the internet is that all this distraction and interruption is immensely profitable. Web companies like to boast about [&hellip;], or offering services that let you [...] "share the things you love with the world" and so on. But the real way to '''build''' a successful online business is to be better than your rivals at undermining people's control of their own attention.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220325214348/https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2013/may/10/conscious-computing-twitter-facebook-google}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[increase]] or [[strengthen]] (something) by adding gradually to it.
#: {{ux|en|We need to '''build''' an income stream over time.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-07-20|volume=408|issue=8845|magazine=w:The Economist|url=http://www.economist.com/news/business/21582001-army-new-online-courses-scaring-wits-out-traditional-universities-can-they|title=The attack of the MOOCs|passage=Since the launch early last year of&nbsp;[&hellip;] two Silicon Valley start-ups offering free education through MOOCs, massive open online courses, the ivory towers of academia have been shaken to their foundations. University brands '''built''' in some cases over centuries have been forced to contemplate the possibility that information technology will rapidly make their existing business model obsolete.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140102182227/http://www.economist.com/news/business/21582001-army-new-online-courses-scaring-wits-out-traditional-universities-can-they}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2026-09-08|work=All That's Interesting|author=Ella Spitz;Kaleena Fraga|title=Christopher Amoroso, The Port Authority Policeman Who Died Rescuing Others On 9/11|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260909185503/https://allthatsinteresting.com/christopher-amoroso|text=Meanwhile, Amoroso was also '''building''' his family. He married his wife Jaime in November 1999, and the couple, along with their baby daughter Sophia Rose, lived in Huguenot on Staten Island.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[establish]] a [[basis]] for (something).
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To form by combining materials or parts.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To develop in [[magnitude]] or [[extent]].
#: {{ux|en|The tension '''built''' between the sparring sisters.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To construct (software) by [[compile|compiling]] its [[source code]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|computing|of [[source code]]}} To be converted into software by [[compilation]], usually with minimal human intervention.
#: {{ux|en|This code won’t '''build''' any more. Have you made any changes?}}

