===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰrewh₁-}}
{{etymon|en|id=fire|:inh|enm:brennen|tree=1}}
From late {{inh|en|enm|burne}}, {{m|enm|birne}}, {{glossary|metathesis|metathetic}} form of {{m|enm|brinne}}, {{m|enm|brynne}}, a variant of {{m|enm|brennen}}, from {{der|en|non|brinna}}, {{m|non|brenna||to burn}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*brinnaną|t=to burn}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰrenw-}}, present stem from {{m|ine-pro|*bʰrewh₁-|t=to boil}}. {{doublet|en|brew}}.

The late metathesis of East Midland ''brin-'' to ''birn-'' (and subsequently ''burn-'') parallels the phonological development of {{m|en|bird}} and {{m|en|dirt}} from {{m|enm|brid}} and {{m|enm|drit}}. As this metathetic form became the Chancery Standard, it displaced the semantic functions and weak conjugation paradigm of the native {{cog|ang|bærnan}} (weak), alongside the surviving remnants of {{m|ang|byrnan}} (strong).
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|cim|prönnan|t=to burn}}, {{cog|nl|barnen}}, {{m|nl|branden|t=to burn}}, {{cog|de|brinnen|t=to burn}}, {{cog|lb|brennen|t=to burn}}, {{cog|wym|brīn|t=to burn}}, {{cog|yi|ברענען|t=to burn}}, {{cog|da|brænde|t=to burn}}, {{cog|fo,is|brenna|t=to burn}}, {{cog|nb|brenne|t=to burn}}, {{cog|nn|brenna}}, {{m|nn|brenne|t=to burn}}, {{cog|sv|brinna|t=to burn}}, {{cog|got|𐌱𐍂𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽|t=to burn}}; also {{cog|kw|bros|t=blazing, boiling}}, {{cog|ga|bruth|t=heat; eruption; surf}}, {{cog|cy|brwd|t=eager, keen; passionate, zealous}}, {{cog|la|defrutum|t=grape must reduced by boiling}}, {{cog|grc|πορφύρω|t=to boil, heave}}, {{cog|sq|burth||{{taxlink|Cyclamen hederifolium|species}}; mouth burning}}, {{cog|hy|աղբյուր|t=fountain, spring}}, {{cog|sa|भुर्वन्|t=runnel, spring}}. More at {{m|en|brew}}.
