##* {{quote-text|en|year=1860|author=w:Henry David Thoreau|title=w:The Last Days of John Brown|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/70123/pg70123-images.html#Page_278|passage=Not being accustomed to make fine distinctions, or to appreciate magnanimity, they read his letters and speeches as if they read them not. They were not aware when they approached a heroic statement,—they did not know when they '''burned'''.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative|US}} To desire, [[yearn]], or [[ache for]] (something); to be intense, as a desire.
##: {{ux|en|I '''burn''' for her touch.}}
##: {{ux|en|He tried to get over it, but he '''burned''' for revenge against those who wronged him.}}
##: {{ux|en|Her need for a new life '''burned''' within her.}}
## {{lb|en|physics|of an element}} To be converted to another [[element]] in a [[nuclear fusion]] reaction, especially in a [[star]]; to undergo [[nuclear fusion]] or [[nuclear fission]].
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To warm (something); to put off intense heat; to be hot.
#: {{ux|en|The heat from the open oven '''burned''' the air around me.}}
#: {{ux|en|The sun's rays '''burned''' hot today.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''burning''' sand hurt my feet.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} To give off [[light]]; to be lit up; to shine so bright as to seem to harm (something).
##: {{ux|en|That bulb has been '''burning''' for years.}}
##: {{ux|en|The spotlight '''burned''' my eyes.}}
##* {{RQ:Bramah Eyes of Max Carrados|passage=I knew that if a light was '''burning''' I should be able to see the window lit up from the yard at the back, although the gas itself would be out of sight.}}
## To [[sunburn]].
##: {{ux|en|She forgot to put on sunscreen and '''burned'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|The harsh summer sun '''burned''' his skin.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[irritate]]; to be or become [[angry]] or [[upset]].
##: {{ux|en|After you said that, she was '''burning''' all afternoon.}}
##: {{ux|en|Your comments got him '''burning'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|That really '''burns''' me up.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to be [[consume]]d by [[fire]]; to [[destroy]] or otherwise [[impact]] with fire or flames.
#: {{syn|en|burn|cinder|forsweal|incinerate|singe|torch|Thesaurus:incinerate}}
#: {{ux|en|We'll '''burn''' all this wood by morning.}}
#: {{ux|en|The campfire was ready to go, so she burned it with her lighter.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''burned''' his manuscript in the fireplace.}}
#* {{RQ:Froissart Berners Cronycles|year=1970|volume=1|chapter=Howe The Englysshmen that were Sent in to Bretayne&#11805; were Tourmented on the See: And Howe the Gaũtoise Desyred to haue the Erle of Flaunders Their Lorde&#11805; to Cõe Dwell in Their Towne of Gaunt|folio=ccxxxiii|passage=They haue ſlayne my baylye&#11805; and diſtroyed the houſes of my men&#11805; banyſſhed and chaſed away myne offycers, and '''brent''' y&#868; houſe in the worlde that I loued beſt.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1976
|first=David B.
|last=Chan
|chapter=The Fall of Nanking
|title=The Usurpation of the Prince of Yen, 1398-1402
|series=Chinese Materials and Research Aids Service Center, Inc. Occasional Series
|issue=7
|issueurl=https://archive.org/details/usurpationofprin0000davi/
|location=San Francisco
|publisher=Chinese Materials Center, Inc.
|oclc=2436871
|page=83
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/usurpationofprin0000davi/page/83/
|text=On July 1, 1402, when the Prince of Yen reached P’u-k’ou (in Kiangsu), Sheng Yung and his imperial troops attacked him and attempted to '''burn''' his boats.⁹⁶}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=29 June 2013|title=Unspontaneous combustion|url=http://www.economist.com/news/asia/21580154-forest-fires-bring-record-levels-air-pollution-and-end-not-sight-unspontaneous|volume=407|issue=8842|page=29|passage=Since the mid-1980s, when Indonesia first began to clear its bountiful forests on an industrial scale in favour of lucrative palm-oil plantations, “haze” has become an almost annual occurrence in South-East Asia. The cheapest way to clear logged woodland is to '''burn''' it, producing an acrid cloud of foul white smoke that, carried by the wind, can cover hundreds, or even thousands, of square miles.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=30 April 2017|speaker=w:Norm Macdonald|title=w:Norm Macdonald Live|season=3|number=12|passage=I've never been on top of the latest trends. When everyone was burning CDs I was still '''burning''' books.}}
## To [[ignite]] (something); to light and smoke (a cigarette, joint, etc.).
##: {{ux|en|She '''burned''' a cigarette with her lighter.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'm going to '''burn''' the stove now so the room will start warming up.}}
## To overheat so as to make unusable.
##: {{ux|en|He '''burned''' the toast.}}
##: {{ux|en|The blacksmith '''burned''' the steel.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Railroad_Gazette/l4QxAQAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22the+flutter%22+steam+boiler&pg=PA352&printsec=frontcover|page=352|journal=Railroad Gazette|volume=6|year=1874|passage=with a heavy fire in the fire box there would be great danger of overheating, and thus injuring the boiler or of "'''burning'''" it, as it is ordinarily termed.}}
## To kill by fire.
##: {{ux|en|She was '''burned''' at the stake.}}
## To [[transform]] using fire.
##: {{ux|en|The factory will '''burn''' that wood into charcoal.}}
## To [[use up]]; to use by burning; to consume as fuel.
##: {{syn|en|fritter|squander}}
##: {{ux|en|I need to '''burn''' some calories.}}
##: {{ux|en|We can '''burn''' a candle tonight.}}
##: {{ux|en|That power plant '''burns''' natural gas.}}
### To [[waste]] [[time]], [[money]] or other [[resource]]s.
###: {{ux|en|We have an hour to '''burn'''.}}
###: {{ux|en|The company has '''burned''' more than a million dollars a month this year.}}
###* {{c.|1897}}, anonymous (lyrics and music), “{{w|Hallelujah, I'm a Bum|Hallelujah, I’m a Bum}}”:
###*: Oh, why don’t you save all the money you earn? / If I didn’t eat, I​’d have money to '''burn'''.
## {{lb|en|of an engine}} To lose [[motor oil]] because it leaks into the combustion chamber and ignites along with the fuel.
##: {{ux|en|I think my car is '''burning''' oil.}}
## {{lb|en|surgery}} To [[cauterize]].
##: {{ux|en|I had to '''burn''' the wound to stop the bleeding.}}
## {{lb|en|pottery}} To [[harden]] (bricks, pottery, etc.) in a [[kiln]].
##: {{syn|en|fire}}
## To [[injure]] (a person or animal) with [[heat]], [[flame]]s or [[scorch]]ing, or by analogous means.
##: {{syn|en|scathe|vitriolize}}
##: {{ux|en|She '''burned''' the child with an iron, and was jailed for ten years.}}
### {{lb|en|slang|figurative}} To [[insult]] or [[defeat]].
###: {{ux|en|I just '''burned''' you again.}}
## To make or produce by the application of real or figurative fire, light or burning heat.
##: {{co|en|to '''burn''' a hole;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; to '''burn''' letters into a block}}
##: {{hypo|en|brand}}
##: {{ux|en|The rancher '''burned''' his brand into the cow's hide.}}
##* {{RQ:Asbjornsen Braekstad Tales|page=78|passage=I posted myself near a place where they had been '''burning''' charcoal, and very soon the hare came running past, close to where I was standing.}}
### {{lb|en|computing}} To write data to a permanent [[storage]] [[medium]] like a [[compact]] [[disc]] or a [[ROM]] chip.
###: {{ux|en|We’ll '''burn''' this program onto an EEPROM one hour before the demo begins.}}
###* {{RQ:Guardian|date=22 October 2015|author=Laura Snapes|title=How Arctic Monkeys’ debut single changed the music industry and ‘killed the NME’|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2015/oct/22/arctic-monkeys-debut-single-i-bet-you-look-good-dancefloor|passage=At first they didn’t do anything by design, beyond writing songs and gigging around Sheffield, distributing homemade demo CDs at shows. Their canny friends '''burned''' copies to leave on buses, but more importantly, uploaded them to filesharing sites and set up a MySpace page.}}
###* {{quote-av|en|date=30 April 2017|speaker=w:Norm Macdonald|title=w:Norm Macdonald Live|season=3|number=12|passage=I've never been on top of the latest trends. When everyone was '''burning''' CDs I was still burning books.}}
### {{senseid|en|render subtitles}} {{lb|en|transitive|computing|by extension}} To [[render]] subtitles into a video's content while [[transcoding]] it, making the subtitles part of the image ({{m|en|hardsubs}}).
###: {{ux|en|My old DVD player could play DivX files but didn't recognize the subtitle file, so I had to '''burn''' them in.}}
### {{lb|en|photography|videography}} To make an area of an image darker {{q|when processing [[photograph]]s in a [[darkroom]], this is accomplished by increasing the [[exposure]] of that area to light}}.
###: {{cot|en|dodge}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To consume, damage, or change the condition of, as if by action of fire or heat; to affect as fire or heat does; to injure with [[acid]] or other chemical reactions; to be painful as if by fire or heat; to be hot.
#: {{ux|en|Be careful, as that chemical is acidic enough to '''burn''' you.}}
#: {{ux|en|Habaneros '''burn''' more intensely than most peppers. They will '''burn''' your mouth.}}
#: {{ux|en|My eyes hurt, the cold and dry air makes them '''burn'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|You might have a fever, your forehead is '''burning'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King John|act=V|scene=iii|passage=This tyrant fever '''burns''' me up.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Spanish Fryar|passage=This dry sorrow '''burns''' up all my tears.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1965|title=Amplified Bible|section=James 4:2|passage=You are jealous and covet [what others have] and your desires go unfulfilled; [so] you become murderers. [To hate is to murder as far as your hearts are concerned.] You '''burn''' with envy and anger and are not able to obtain [the gratification, the contentment, and the happiness that you seek], so you fight and war. You do not have, because you do not ask.}}
## {{lb|en|chemistry}} To cause to combine with oxygen or other active agent, with evolution of heat; to consume; to oxidize.
##: {{co|en|to '''burn''' iron in oxygen}}
##: {{ux|en|A human being '''burns''' a certain amount of carbon with each respiration.}}
## {{lb|en|chemistry|dated}} To combine energetically, with evolution of heat.
##: {{ux|en|Copper '''burns''' in chlorine.}}
# ''Senses as to a figurative burning action''
## {{lb|en|sports}} To [[defeat]], [[overcome]] or [[score]] on.
##: {{ux|en|The Rangers '''burned''' the visiting team.}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''burned''' our defenses.}}
##: {{ux|en|With that dunk, he '''burned''' the defender and the coach's whole strategy.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|usually|passive}} To subject to [[misfortune]] or [[maltreatment]]; to [[swindle]] or [[rob]].
##: {{ux|en|I've been '''burned''' before by these kinds of promises.}}
##: {{ux|en|That's when we '''burn''' the mark, and we'll be gone before he knows what happened.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To [[betray]]; to [[con]] or [[rob]].
##: {{syn|en|knife|sell|stab in the back|Thesaurus:betray}}
##: {{ux|en|The informant '''burned''' him.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To [[shoot]] someone with a [[firearm]].
## {{lb|en|transitive|espionage}} To [[compromise]] (a [[cover story]]).
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Thomas H. Cook|title=Night Secrets|passage=He had already '''burned''' his cover with Mrs. Phillips, and it was not a mistake he intended to make again.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=Vanessa Kier|title=Vengeance: The SSU Book 1|passage=Eventually they'd report back to Ryker, and he still didn't know if Ryker had personally '''burned''' his cover and sent assassins after him, or if the SSU had a mole. Until he knew for certain, he had to play this safe.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|espionage}} To [[blackmail]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=John le Carré|title=Smiley's People|passage="''How'' does Leipzig '''burn''' him precisely?" Enderby insisted. "What's the pressure? Dirty pix—well, okay. Karla's a puritan, so's Kirov. But I mean, Christ, this isn't the fifties, is it? {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|cards|pontoon}} To swap a pair of cards for another pair, or to deal a [[dead]] card.
## {{lb|en|intransitive|curling}} To accidentally touch a moving [[stone]].
## {{lb|en|intransitive|slang|card games|gambling}} To [[discard]].

