# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[place#Verb|place]] inside a [[#Noun|box]]; to [[pack#Verb|pack]] in one or more boxes.
#* {{quote-journal |en-US |text=He also testified that the reason he crated the one machine and '''boxed''' the other was because one machine was stronger and the other frailer, and that made it dangerous to crate it. |page=71 |title=Edwin B. Stimpson Co. v. Chicago, B. & Q. R. Co. |work=New York Supplement |volume=164 |year=1917}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Karen Motylewski|chapter=Surveying Your Own Institution: What Do You Need to Know?| title=What an Institution Can Do to Survey Its Own Preservation Needs| series=Technical Leaflet: General Preservation| seriesvolume=508-470-1010|location=Andover, Mass.|publisher=w:Northeast Document Conservation Center| month=August| year=1991| section=section V.D.6 (Scrapbooks and Ephemera)| page=21| oclc=25808350| newversion=reprinted in| 2ndauthor=Sherry Byrne| title2=Collection Maintenance and Improvement| series2=Preservation Planning Program| location2=Washington, D.C.| publisher2=w:Association of Research Libraries| year2=1993| page2=87| pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=TbbjAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA87| isbn2=978-0-918006-62-2| passage=Scrapbooks that have enduring value in their original form should be individually '''boxed''' in custom-fitted boxes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:B. J. Daniels (writer)|B. J. Daniels]]|chapter=Gun-shy Bride| chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=1LUtDwAAQBAJ&pg=PT8| title=Cold Justice| edition=2nd Australian paperback| location=Sydney, N.S.W.| publisher=[[w:Mills & Boon|Harlequin Mills & Boon]]| year=2017| section=chapter 1| isbn=978-1-4892-4556-4| passage="I best get busy and '''box''' up these bones," she said, suddenly anxious to get moving. {{...}} As she started to step around the grave washed out by last night's rainstorm, the sun caught on something caught in the mud.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} ''Usually followed by'' '''{{l|en|in}}''': to [[surround]] and [[enclose]] in a way that [[restrict]]s [[movement]]; to [[corner#Verb|corner]], to [[hem in]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Bill Borcherdt| chapter=The Door Swings Both Ways: When Children Double Bind Their Parents| title=Making Families Work and What to Do when They Don’t: Thirty Guides for Imperfect Parents of Imperfect Children| series=Haworth Marriage and the Family| location=New York, N.Y.| publisher=[[w:Haworth Press|The Haworth Press]]| year=1996| isbn=978-0-7890-0073-6| location2=Binghamton, N.Y.| publisher2=The Haworth Press| year2=2007| page2=65| pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=-BCqDpXuwsYC&pg=PA65| isbn2=978-0-7890-0127-6| passage=A large majority of children seem to delight in emotionally '''boxing''' in their parents—setting the double-bind trap by giving the parent two choices but determining ahead of time that neither choice will be sufficient for their satisfaction.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[mix#Verb|mix]] two [[container]]s of [[paint#Noun|paint]] of [[similar]] [[colour#Noun|colour]] to [[ensure]] that the color is [[identical]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Brian Santos|chapter=Painting Like a Pro|title=Painting Secrets from Brian Santos, the Wall Wizard| location=Des Moines, Iowa| publisher=[[w:Meredith Corporation|Meredith Books]]| year=2004| page=95| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=RVi55qBC1REC&pg=PA95| isbn=978-0-696-21759-3| passage=Straining eliminates lumps in the paint. If the paint has separated, stir the thick paint up from the bottom of each can to free as many lumps as possible. Then '''box''' the paint, pouring it all together through a nylon paint strainer and into the bucket. Paint less than one year old usually doesn't require straining. Older paint might have a thick skin on the top; remove the skin and set it aside. '''Box''' the paint, pouring it through a nylon paint strainer into the bucket.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|agriculture}} To make an [[incision]] or [[hole#Noun|hole]] in (a [[tree]]) for the purpose of [[procure|procuring]] the [[sap#Noun|sap]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=F. L. B.|title=The Maple Sugar Industry| magazine=Forest Leaves| location=Philadelphia, Pa.| publisher=Pennsylvania Forestry Association| month=April| year=1918| volume=XVI| issue=8 (number 184 overall)| page=115| pageurl=https://archive.org/details/forestleavesv1600penn/page/n88/mode/1up| column=2| oclc=38266006| passage=The early settlers either '''boxed''' the tree or cut large slanting gashes, from the lower end of which a rudely fashioned spout conducted the sap to a bucket. This method was very destructive to the tree, and boring was substituted for it.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|architecture}} To [[enclose]] with [[boarding#Noun|boarding]], [[lathing#Noun|lathing]], etc., so as to [[conceal]] (for example, [[pipe#Noun|pipes]]) or to bring to a required [[form#Noun|form]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Ronald V. Clarke|Ronald V[ictor] Clarke]]; [[w:David Lester (psychologist)|David Lester]]| chapter=Introduction to the Transaction Edition| title=Suicide: Closing the Exits| location=New Brunswick, N.J.| publisher=w:Transaction Publishers| year=2013| page=ix| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ifnvAQAAQBAJ&pg=PR9| isbn=978-1-4128-5169-5| passage=As early as the 1850s, prisons were being made "safer" by '''boxing''' in water pipes and enclosing galleries with netting to prevent jumping.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|engineering}} To [[furnish]] (for example, the [[axle]] of a [[wheel#Noun|wheel]]) with a box.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Sir Archibald Alison, 1st Baronet|Archibald Alison]] (judge)| title={{smallcaps|Sarah Hamil}}, or {{smallcaps|Docherty}}, relict of the deceased {{smallcaps|Daniel Docherty, Agnes Docherty}}, and {{smallcaps|Sarah Docherty}}, residing with her, his daughters and only children, ''v.'' {{smallcaps|James Alexander}}, Glasgow, Calenderer, defender| magazine=The Scottish Law Magazine and Sheriff Court Reporter| location=Glasgow| publisher=Thomas Murray & Son,{{nb...|49 Buchanan Street}}; Edinburgh: Maclachlan and Stewart| date=25 February 1862| year_published=December 1862| volume=I (New Series)| page=41| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BA85AQAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA41| column=1| oclc=841742918| passage=[T]he death of the said deceased Daniel Docherty, while in the defender's employment as an engineman, {{...}} is alleged to have been owing to the engine house, which contained the engine of which the deceased had charge, being of a dangerous and improper construction, and the fly-wheel not having been '''boxed''' in or covered: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|graphic design|printing}} To enclose ([[image#Noun|images]], [[text#Noun|text]], etc.) in a box.
# {{lb|en|transitive|object-oriented programming}} To place a [[value#Noun|value]] of a [[primitive type]] into a casing [[object#Noun|object]].
# {{lb|en|motorsport}} To enter the [[pit]].
