# {{senseid|en|tree}} Any of various [[evergreen]] [[shrub]]s or [[tree]]s of [[genus]] {{taxfmt|Buxus|genus}}, especially [[common box]], {{vern|European box}}, or [[boxwood]] ({{taxfmt|Buxus sempervirens|species}}) which is often used for making [[hedge#Noun|hedges]] and [[topiary]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1587|author=Leuinus Lemnius|translator=Thomas Newton|title=An Herball to the Bible{{...}}|location=London|publisher=Edmund Bollifant|url=https://www.google.co.nz/books/edition/An_Herbal_for_the_Bible_Containing_a_pla/fKxkAAAAcAAJ|page=207|passage=And no maruell. For, the leaues of '''Boxe''' be [[deletorious]], poiſonous, deadlie, and to the bodie of man very noiſome, dangerous and peſtilent{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|volume=II|chapter=V|pages=130–131|pageref=130|passage=He strayed down a walk edged with '''box'''; with apple trees, pear trees, and cherry trees on one side, and a border on the other, full of all sorts of old-fashioned flowers, stocks, sweet-williams, primroses, pansies, mingled with southernwood, sweet-briar, and various fragrant herbs.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ambra Edwards|title=Topiary: We're all going bonkers about box [print version: Bonkers about box, 22 November 2014, page&nbsp;G3]|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/gardening/plants/11238853/Topiary-were-all-going-bonkers-about-box.html|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Gardening)|date=19 November 2014|passage="'''Box''' makes a statement without having to do much: just trim twice a year and keep it weeded. It's a bit of a lazy gardener's plant." This, no doubt, is what makes '''box''' so popular with show home developers and city dwellers&nbsp;– there is scarce a balcony or front door anywhere that cannot be improved by a '''box''' ball in a pot.}}
