===Etymology===
{{dercat|sco|gmw-pro|gem-pro|inh=2}}
From {{der|sco|enm|broken|bro(o)ken|to use, enjoy, digest}}, from {{der|sco|ang|brūcan||to use, enjoy}}. See also {{l|sco|brouk}}.

===Verb===
{{head|sco|verb|head=tae brook}}

# To [[enjoy]]; to [[possess]]; to have [[use]] or [[owndom]] of.

{{also|Breeze}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɹiːz/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-breeze.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-breeze.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːz|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From the earlier (nautical) term {{m|en|brise}}, {{m|en|brize|t=breeze}}, from {{inh|en|enm|brees|t=wind}}. Ultimate origin {{unk|en|title=obscure}}.

Variously supposed to derive from a {{der|en|gem|-}} source like {{cog|stq|Briese||breeze}}, {{cog|fy|brys|t=a cool wind}}, {{cog|nl|bries|t=breeze}}, early {{cog|nl|brysen|t=to blow cool and fresh}}, or from {{cog|es|brisa||northeast wind}}.<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref><ref>{{R:Merriam Webster Online}}</ref>

The earliest attestations are in {{cog|enm|brees}} (1460), {{cog|ca|brisa}}, and {{cog|it|brezza}} (all in 15th century), with Spanish (1504) and {{cog|pt|briza}} (16th century) following closely after. The aforementioned Dutch cognates and {{cog|fr|brise}}, however, are attested later than the term in English. The only internal hypothesis for any of those languages is a modification of {{noncog|pro|bisa|t=strong wind}}, which is not widely accepted.

Compare also {{noncog|sq|breshër||hail}}.

