===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To prepare for something bad, such as an impact or blow.
#: {{ux|en|All hands, '''brace''' for impact!}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Brace''' yourself!}}
#: {{ux|en|The boy has no idea about everything that's been going on. You need to '''brace''' him for what's about to happen.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=January 22, 2013|author=Phil McNulty|title=Aston Villa 2-1 Bradford (3-4)| work=BBC| url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/21022502|passage=Bradford would have been '''braced''' for an early assault from Villa as they tried to cut the deficit - and so it proved as they struggled to control the physical presence and aerial threat of Benteke, who headed straight at Bradford keeper Matt Duke when he should have done better.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160124024927/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/21022502|archivedate=24 January 2016}}
# To place in a position for resisting pressure; to hold firmly.
#: {{ux|en|He '''braced''' himself against the crowd.}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''braced''' her back against the wall.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1845|author=Edward Fairfax (tr.)|title=Godfrey of Bulloigne; or, The Recovery of Jerusalem: Done into English Heroical Verse|passage=A sturdy lance in his right hand he '''braced'''.}}
